---
// apps/site/src/pages/network.astro
// Why a directory of inboxes matters: how a business joins, how receipts become reputation on
// both sides, and what the network is never allowed to see. LiveMetrics carries the live numbers
// so the fetch, the fallback and the refresh live in one place.
import LiveMetrics from "../components/LiveMetrics.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const contents = [
  { href: "#directory", label: "The directory" },
  { href: "#receipts", label: "Receipts, not reviews" },
  { href: "#clients", label: "Good clients" },
  { href: "#ranking", label: "How the best win" },
  { href: "#privacy", label: "What it never sees" },
  { href: "#join", label: "Join" },
];

const signals = [
  {
    title: "A customer you know",
    text: "Their record is clean and their agent is verified. A rule confirms the booking the moment it arrives, at any hour.",
    tint: "success",
  },
  {
    title: "A first-timer",
    text: "No record yet, and nothing against them. A rule asks for a deposit, or holds the slot and sends the request to you.",
    tint: "warning",
  },
  {
    title: "Anything unusual",
    text: "A record with late cancellations behind it, or a request that does not fit your limits. It goes to a person, with the signal shown beside it.",
    tint: "message",
  },
];

const never = [
  {
    k: "Stays in your instance",
    v: "Conversations, prices, names, addresses, payment references, every item and every event.",
  },
  {
    k: "Reaches the network",
    v: "Your domain, the version and runtime the instance reports, and hourly counts in four buckets. Later, the public manifest at your domain, receipt signatures and reviews.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="The network · Surfing Dog"
  description="A directory of inboxes that agents can search, where listing is by verified domain and ranking follows kept promises. How receipts will become reputation on both sides, how a business will know its good clients, and what the network is never allowed to see."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" aria-labelledby="page-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">The network</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="page-h">A directory of inboxes that keep their promises</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every Surfing Dog inbox can join a network: a place where an agent can find a business, and a
      record of promises kept on both sides of the exchange. It is off until you switch it on, and
      it never sees what your customers write.
    </p>
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
      {contents.map((c) => <a class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm" href={c.href}>{c.label}</a>)}
    </nav>
  </section>

  <LiveMetrics />

  <section class="section" id="directory" aria-labelledby="directory-h" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap wrap-read">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Membership</p>
          <h2 class="h-section" id="directory-h">One directory, open format</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            An inbox joins by registering its own domain. After that it reports counts, once an
            hour, and nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="says">
        <p>
          Membership ships today. Switch it on and your instance registers its domain with the
          network and sends an hourly ping: the version it runs, what it runs on, and how many
          bookings, orders, quote requests and messages arrived in the last day. Counts only. No
          content, no names, no amounts. Test items are left out.
        </p>
        <p>
          The directory that lists those instances is a separate service, and it is being built.
          <span class="pill">Coming</span> It will verify a domain before listing it, by fetching
          the manifest at that address and checking that the instance names itself. A listing will
          carry the business name, where it is, what it does, the languages it answers in, and the
          doors it offers.
        </p>
        <p>
          surfingdog.ai runs the first network. The protocol is two HTTP calls, so a trade body, a
          city or a franchise can run one of their own, and an inbox will be able to belong to more
          than one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="receipts" aria-labelledby="receipts-h" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap wrap-read">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Reputation</p>
          <h2 class="h-section" id="receipts-h">Receipts, not reviews</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            A review should be proof that something happened, not an opinion anyone can post about
            anyone.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="pill">Coming</span>
      </div>

      <div class="says">
        <p>
          When an item completes, the instance will issue a receipt: a small signed record of what
          was agreed and what happened. The customer's agent will counter-sign it, so both sides end
          up holding the same proof, and neither can quietly change it afterwards.
        </p>
        <p>
          A review will only be accepted against a receipt, in both directions. Nobody will be able
          to review a business they never dealt with, and no business will be able to review a
          customer it never served.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both reviews will be sealed when they are written and revealed together when the window
          closes. Neither side sees the other's words in time to react to them, which is the only
          way honest feedback survives contact with a rating.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="more">
        <a class="link" href="/blog/receipts-not-reviews/">Read why we are starting with receipts</a>
      </p>
      <p class="fine">
        Today the endpoint and the MCP tool exist and answer that receipts are not ready. Nothing
        on this page is issuing a receipt yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="clients" aria-labelledby="clients-h" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap wrap-read">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Both sides</p>
          <h2 class="h-section" id="clients-h">Knowing who the good clients are</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Businesses have been rated by their customers for twenty years. The other half of that
            record has never existed.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="pill">Coming</span>
      </div>

      <div class="says">
        <p>
          Receipts add up. Over time a customer, or the agent acting for them, will carry a record:
          bookings kept, bookings cancelled late, no-shows, orders paid, disputes raised.
        </p>
        <p>
          When a request arrives you will see that record as a short signal, something like
          <span class="mono">verified, 14 receipts, 13 kept</span>. It will be advice, never a
          verdict. The software will not decline anyone on its own, and a person will be able to see
          what is held about them and contest it.
        </p>
        <p>
          In practice it changes what your rules can safely do on their own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="grid-cards cards three" data-reveal="stagger">
        {
          signals.map((s) => (
            <li class="glass card">
              <span class:list={["pill", `tint-${s.tint}`]}>
                <i class:list={["dot", `dot-${s.tint}`]} />
                {s.title}
              </span>
              <p class="card-text">{s.text}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <p class="fine">
        The incentive runs both ways. An agent that keeps the bookings it makes earns faster
        confirmations for the people it acts for, so behaving well is worth more to it than pushing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="ranking" aria-labelledby="ranking-h" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap wrap-read">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Discovery</p>
          <h2 class="h-section" id="ranking-h">How the best businesses win</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Listing by verified domain, ranking by kept promises. Neither can be bought.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="pill">Coming</span>
      </div>

      <div class="says">
        <p>
          Every entry will be a real business at a domain it proved it controls, so the directory
          cannot fill up with listings nobody stands behind.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ranking will follow what a business actually did: confirmations honoured, quotes answered,
          orders fulfilled, receipts issued, and how quickly someone replies. Not advertising spend,
          and not the number of reviews collected.
        </p>
        <p>
          Agents will find businesses through the directory and prefer the ones whose doors work and
          whose record is clean. A good small business gets found the same way a large one does,
          because the thing being measured is the same in both cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          And because reviews are two-sided and revealed together, there is nothing to retaliate
          against. Honest feedback costs nothing to give, so the businesses that issue receipts and
          answer their reviews will sit above the ones that do not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-h" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap wrap-read">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Limits</p>
          <h2 class="h-section" id="privacy-h">What the network never sees</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            The line is drawn in the code, not in a policy document: counts leave the instance,
            content does not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="glass spec">
        {
          never.map((n) => (
            <div class="row-spec">
              <dt>{n.k}</dt>
              <dd>{n.v}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <div class="says top">
        <p>
          Reputation will be advisory. It will decay with time, so an old mistake stops counting
          against someone forever, and it can be contested by the person it describes.
        </p>
        <p>
          The design follows European data protection rules from the start, including the part that
          matters most here: no decision about a person is made automatically by the software alone.
          A refusal is always a person's refusal, or a rule that person wrote and can read back in
          one sentence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="join" aria-labelledby="join-h" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap wrap-read">
      <div class="sec-head">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Switch it on</p>
          <h2 class="h-section" id="join-h">Join, or run your own</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            Membership is a switch in Settings, and it is off until you turn it on.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="says">
        <p>
          Open Settings, switch network membership on, and give the instance its public address. It
          registers itself and starts reporting. Switch it off and the reporting stops the same
          minute.
        </p>
        <p>
          Running a network of your own is two HTTP calls: one that takes a registration, one that
          takes the hourly ping. The payloads are open, and so is everything that reads them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="glass panel cta">
        <p class="position">
          <span class="u-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>Your inbox first. The network is worth joining once it is answering for you.</span>
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#run">Get your inbox</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/technical/">Read the technical page</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .contents {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    margin-top: var(--sp-6);
  }

  .says {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-4);
  }
  .says.top {
    margin-top: var(--sp-6);
  }
  .says p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-body);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .says .pill {
    margin-inline: var(--sp-1);
  }
  .says .mono {
    font-size: 0.92em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .more {
    margin: var(--sp-5) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-card);
  }

  .cards {
    margin: var(--sp-6) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .cards.three {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  .card .card-text {
    margin-top: var(--sp-3);
  }

  .spec {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--sp-1) var(--sp-5);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  }
  .row-spec {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 220px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--sp-1) var(--sp-4);
    padding: var(--sp-4) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }
  .row-spec:first-child {
    border-top: 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 660px) {
    .row-spec {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--sp-1);
    }
  }
  .spec dt {
    font-size: var(--fs-card);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .spec dd {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure-card);
    font-size: var(--fs-card);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .cta {
    margin-top: var(--sp-6);
  }
  .position {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 44px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--sp-4);
    align-items: center;
    max-width: 54ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-body);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: var(--lh-ui);
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--sp-2) var(--sp-3);
    margin-top: var(--sp-5);
  }
</style>
